  "body": "Given the following web-platform-tests `mimesniff/test.py`:\r\n```python\r\ndef main(request, response):\r\n    response.add_required_headers = False\r\n    response.writer.write_header(\"X-Content-Type-Options\", \"nosniff\")\r\n    response.writer.write_content(open(\"images/blue.png\").read()) # I don't understand why this path works\r\n```\r\nSafari will download the response, Chrome will render it as text, and Firefox will show an image.\r\n\r\nThis strongly suggests that step 1 and 2 of https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#mime-type-sniffing-algorithm should switch places, but before we could make such a change #25 needs more work.\r\n\r\nHowever, note that if they switch places that would also render the \"sniff-scriptable flag\" obsolete. It's not clear to me if that matters though, originally the \"scriptable\" annotation was there because everything was part of the same table: https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-websec-mime-sniff. (That might still be a better approach, it isn't clear that what we have now is a good match for how implementations function.)",
